## A B S T R A C T
Plant flavonoids are secondary metabolites containing a benzo- $\cdot\gamma$ -pyrone structure, which are widely present in plants and have a variety of physiological and pharmacological activities. However, current flavonoid production from plant extraction or chemical synthesis does not meet the requirements of green and sustainable develop­ ment. Fortunately, microbial synthesis of flavonoids has shown the potential for large-scale production with the advantages of being controllable and environmentally friendly, and a variety of microorganisms have been developed as microbial cell factories (MCFs) to synthesize plant flavonoids owing to the feasibility of genetic manipulations. However, most of MCFs have not yet been commercialized and industrialized because of the challenges posed by unbalanced metabolic flux among various pathways and conflict between cell growth and production. Here, strategies for coping with the challenges are summarized in terms of enzymes, pathways, metabolic networks, host cells. And combined with protein structure prediction, de novo protein design, artificial intelligence (AI), biocatalytic retrosynthesis, and intelligent stress resistance, it provides new insights for the high efficient production of plant flavonoids and other plant natural products in MCFs.
## 1. Introduction
Plant flavonoids are a type of secondary metabolite widely found in plants, with antioxidant (Yao et al., 2004), anti-inflammatory, anti-­ cancer (Hai et al., 2010; Middleton et al., 2000; Raffa et al., 2017), and anticoagulation properties (Lin et al., 2013). Additionally, they play a role in the prevention and treatment of cardiovascular, cerebrovascular diseases (Rice-Evans et al., 1996), obesity, and diabetes (Bedekar et al., 2010; Chemler et al., 2007; Leonard et al., 2008), making them useful in the production of medicine, cosmetics, and food (Can Karaca et al., 2015; Lam and Nickerson, 2013; McClements et al., 2017; Sun et al., 2019). However, the current methods for obtaining flavonoids include plant extraction or chemical synthesis. The disadvantages of plant extraction of flavonoids include high production cost, high energy consumption depending on low flavonoid content, and the composition of plant cultivation being heavily influenced by changes in climate. Chemical synthesis of flavonoids usually involves long reaction steps, harsh reaction conditions, and toxic chemical reagents, which make them unsuitable for the requirements of sustainable development and market supply of plant flavonoids.
Fortunately, recent advances in omics technology and synthetic biology have greatly promoted the illustration of biosynthetic pathways and the mining of key enzymes for plant flavonoids. Many plant flavo­ noids such as naringenin, kaempferol, and liquiritigenin (Table 1) have been synthesized in microbial cell factories (MCFs). Although the flavonoid production approach is available, it still faces the challenges posed by unbalanced carbon flux among various pathways and conflict between cell growth and production (Fig. 2B), which seriously hampers their commercial production. Therefore, many novel strategies have been developed to continuously innovate the synthesis methods and yield of plant flavonoids. This review comprehensively elaborates on the challenges of flavonoid synthesis in MCFs. And it summarizes the stra­ tegies for coping with the challenges in terms of the mining and modi­ fication of key enzymes, construction and optimization of metabolic pathways, regulation and control of metabolic networks, and selection and design of host cells. Finally, a series of new approaches are discussed to mitigate these challenges by using protein structure prediction, de novo protein design, artificial intelligence (AI), biocatalytic retrosyn­ thesis, and intelligent stress resistance to provide new insights and op­ portunities for the high efficient production of plant flavonoids.
## 2. Challenges for high efficient production of plant flavonoids in MCFs
There are two primary challenges in metabolic engineering: the unbalanced metabolic flux among various metabolic pathways and the conflict between cell growth and production (Fig. 2 B). Although the biosynthetic pathway of plant flavonoids has been intensively studied (Fig. 1) and many MCFs have been successfully constructed to synthesize plant flavonoids (Table 1), they still face the same challenges in indus­ trial production and application.
## 2.1. Unbalanced metabolic flux in MCFs
One of the main challenges in using MCFs to synthesize plant fla­ vonoids is the unbalanced metabolic flux, resulting in low synthesis ef­ ficiency and low yield. As shown in Table 1, the production of most plant flavonoids is limited to the mg level, hampering the industrial exploi­ tation of these strains. For example, liquiritigenin (Yan et al., 2007) and silybin (Yang et al., 2020b) were only synthesized at final titers of 17.1 $\mathrm{mg/L}$ and $104.9\mathrm{mg/L}$ and did not meet the requirements of industrial production. The main reasons for these challenges are the lack of en­ zymes with high efficiency catalytic activity or high specificity in metabolic pathways, suggesting that a larger metabolic flux is needed for the synthesis of plant flavonoids (Pandey et al., 2016) (Fig. 2A). The competitive pathways that shunt metabolic flux in MCFs are also the main reason for the unbalanced metabolic flux, such as the competition of malonyl coenzyme A (CoA) between the heterologous metabolic pathway and endogenous metabolic networks, resulting in low meta­ bolic flux for naringenin (Fowler et al., 2009)(Fig. 2A). The different hosts used for flavonoid production have different synthesis abilities, including membrane structure, protein production, and cofactor content (Delmulle et al., 2018; Marsafari et al., 2020). For example, Yarrowia lipolytica is considered an ideal host cell for the synthesis of various flavonoids because of its vigorous cell growth and high acetyl-CoA and malonyl-CoA flux (Muhammad et al., 2020). Therefore, the unbalanced metabolic flux in MCFs seriously hinders their industrial application.
## 2.2. Conflict between cell growth and production in MCFs
Another major challenge is the conflict between cell growth and production. The overexpression of heterologous or endogenous meta­ bolic pathways and the accumulation of heterologous proteins, pathway intermediates, and products affect the normal metabolic process of MCFs, leading to metabolic burden and disorder (Gao et al., 2021) (Fig. 2C). For example, the synthesis of naringenin (Santos et al., 2011), kaempferol (Rodriguez et al., 2017), and afzelechin (Jones et al., 2017) requires the overexpression of heterologous genes in Escherichia coli and Saccharomyces cerevisiae, leading to cell growth retardation and low productivity. In addition, as shown in Table 1, the synthesis of most plant flavonoids in MCFs requires exogenous addition of $p$ -coumaric acid, phenylalanine, or tyrosine (Leonard et al., 2005, 2006). Their exogenous addition also affected the normal metabolism and growth of MCFs, and they are expensive compared with commonly used fermen­ tation substrates (glucose), which increases the production cost and does not meet the requirements of industrial production. Although the MCFs that could synthesize the $p$ -coumaric acid (Liu et al., 2019), phenylala­ nine (Rodriguez et al., 2015), or tyrosine (Liu et al., 2019) from glucose were constructed, they all required the overexpression of endogenous metabolic pathways, leading to growth retardation and low production efficiency of MCFs (Fig. 2C). Therefore, the conflict between cell growth and production in MCFs severely restricts their industrial application.
## 3. Strategies for solving these challenges
To cope with the challenges posed by unbalanced metabolic flux among various metabolic pathways and conflict between cell growth and production, a variety of strategies have been adopted in terms of enzymes, pathways, metabolic networks, and host cells.
## 3.1. Mining and modifying of key enzymes
The exploitation of highly active and highly specific enzymes through function-based gene mining or sequence-based protein design is a powerful tool used to keep the balance of metabolic flux among various metabolic pathways in flavonoid production. Omics technology, such as RNA-seq, provides an opportunity to facilitate potential func­ tional prediction by data integration. The genomes or transcriptomes of plants that are rich in specific flavonoids (Song et al., 2017)have been sequenced and analyzed. Flavonoid biosynthetic enzymes for flavonoid synthesis in such plants should be more efficient and highly specific (Fig. 3). For example, Scutellaria baicalensis Georgi, a plant whose active ingredients are mainly flavonoids, such as baicalin or wogonin (Xu et al., 2020b), has been analyzed for its genome and transcriptome. Based on the analysis of transcriptome sequencing, gene expression, and evolu­ tionary trees, a flavone 6-hydroxylase (SbCYP82D1.1) was obtained with an apparent $K_{\mathrm{M}}$ value of $0.121~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and apparent maximal velocity value of 733.047 pkat $\mathbf{m}{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}$ protein, converting chrysin at high effi­ ciency (Zhao et al., 2018). The evolutionary mechanism of effective components in Scutellaria baicalensis Georgi was also analyzed and found that the candidate gene PFOMT5 (O-methyltransferase of phenyl­ propanoid and flavonoid) was the most effective catalyst for the biosynthesis of wogonin, with an apparent $K_{\mathrm{M}}$ of $4.58\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and a maximal velocity of 10.34 nkat $\mathrm{mg^{-1}}$ . In contrast, PFOMT1 and PFOMT2 had lower activity on norwogonin because of their higher $K_{\mathrm{M}}$ values (29.08 $\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and $11.98~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively) for norwogonin and their lower maximal velocities (8.07 nkat $\mathrm{mg^{-1}}$ and 7.75 nkat $\mathrm{mg^{-1}}$ , respectively) (Zhao et al., 2019). In addition, protein engineering is the most widely used method to improve enzyme activity and specificity (Fig. 3). Based on the relationship between the structures of protein molecules and their biological functions, chemical, physical, and molecular biology methods, the rational or irrational design of inefficient proteins is an effective method to improve their activity and specificity. For example, a mutant combination of $S m\mathrm{{F}3^{\prime}\mathrm{{H}^{D285N}}}$ (flavonoid $3^{\prime}$ -hydroxylase from Silybum marianum (L) Gaertn) $/S m C P\mathrm{R}^{\mathrm{I453V}}$ (cytochrome P450 reductase from Silybum marianum (L) Gaertn) was obtained using directional evolution to improve the activity and specificity of $S m\mathrm{{F3^{\prime}H}}/S m\mathrm{{CPR}}$ . Combined with promoter optimization and fermentation optimization, the (2S)-eriodictyol titer was improved to $3.3\mathrm{g/L}$ , which was the highest titer currently reported (Gao et al., 2020b). In addition, analyzing the crystal structures of the enzymes and rational design are also important for building efficient enzymes. For example, 15 previously unreported polyketide (PK) molecules were generated by exploring the substrate promiscuity of the wild-type stilbene synthase-like (STS) enzyme and all mutants using unnatural substrates (Bhan et al., 2015a). Meanwhile, a
