## 3.2. Building of efficient metabolic pathways
With the development of promoter engineering, cofactor engineer­ ing, and coenzyme engineering, the reconstruction of an efficient metabolic pathway and remodeled process engineering have provided new opportunities to increase metabolic flux and produce flavonoids with high titers (Fig. 4). Promoter engineering is a popular method for coordinating the expression of various enzymes and redox systems. For example, by replacing the IPTG-inducible promoter (pT7) with the constitutive gap promoter (pGAP) and allowing the more efficient genes to be expressed under the control of a gap promoter, the addition of inducers was avoided and the gene could be transcribed together as a polycistronic mRNA strand and translated together in the cytoplasm to form a protein complex. This resulted in a final improved titer of $2.3\mathrm{g/L}$ resveratrol in $E.$ . coli (Lim et al., 2011). Additionally, a promoter library had been constructed and screened for the best promoter to increase the naringenin yield to $1.21{\ g}/\mathrm{L}$ , and the (2S)-eriodyctiol titer was improved to $3.3\mathrm{g/L}$ by using a promoter to regulate the redox system (Gao et al., 2020b, 2020c). In addition, cofactor and coenzyme engineering (Fig. 4) were used to provide an appropriate catalytic environment for key en­ zymes in a metabolic pathway. Based on this, two different model ob­ jectives were used to identify possible genotypes that exhibited either improved overall NADPH production or an improved flux through an artificial reaction coupling NADPH yield to biomass, and provided suf­ ficient cofactors for the dihydroflavonol 4-reductase and leucoantho­ cyanidin reductase. The best-engineered strain accumulated up to 817 $\mathrm{mg/L}$ of leucocyanidin and $39~\mathrm{mg/L}$ $(+)$ -catechin, a 4-fold and 2-fold increase, respectively, compared to the wild-type control (Chemler et al., 2010). In addition, indium phosphide nanomaterials combined with S. cerevisiae to construct a biological hybrid system increased the ratio of $\mathrm{NADPH/NADP^{+}}$ to 87.1 under light conditions and provided sufficient cofactors for the reduction of 3-dehydosashikimic acid to shikimic acid (Guo et al., 2018). Many coenzymes in the metabolic pathway have been identified. For example, two chalcone isomerases (CHIL1 and CHIL2) did not have catalytic activity but participated in and regulated the biosynthesis of flavanol in hops, and it was found that CHIL2 could interact with chalcone synthase (CHS) and membrane located isopentenyl transferase (IPT) in the flavanol synthesis pathway, thereby improving their activities (Ban et al., 2018). In conclusion, promoter engineering, redox adaptation, and matching sufficient co­ factors or appropriate coenzymes for key enzymes in a pathway can coordinate the relationship among key enzymes and maintain a good catalytic environment, solving the problem of unbalanced metabolic flux.
## 3.3. Coordinating of the conflicts
The mathematical model and dynamic regulation are effective stra­ tegies for coordinating major conflicts in MCFs. Many researchers have developed a genome-scale metabolic network model, stoichiometric model, and dynamic regulation to increase the supply of precursors and dynamically regulate the relationship between cell growth and pro­ duction (Fig. 5A). For example, an integrated computational and experimental approach was proposed to improve the intracellular availability of malonyl-CoA in E. coli, resulting in a $560\%$ increase in volumetric flavanone production $(474~\mathrm{mg/L})$ . Coupled with an evolu­ tionary search, it demonstrated the utility of a purely stoichiometric network to predict improved $E$ . coli genotypes that more effectively channel carbon flux toward malonyl coenzyme A (CoA) and the specific flavanone production. Finally, the optimally engineered strains were increased by over $660\%$ for naringenin (Fowler et al., 2009). Dynamic regulation was mainly used to coordinate the conflict between cell growth and production in MCFs because of the overexpression of het­ erologous or endogenous metabolic pathways and the accumulation of heterologous proteins, pathway intermediates, and products (Fig. 5B).
This method allows cells to dynamically sense and process specific sig­ nals by designing and constructing gene pathway units and adaptively adjusting them according to the changes in the signals to achieve the best state of product synthesis or target system (Cress et al., 2015). It has been applied in many studies and has achieved a significant increase in the yield of target products (Table 2) (Gao et al., 2019; Zhou et al., 2019a). For example, lipogenic negative autoregulation with metabolite addiction was built to redistribute carbon flux and improve strain sta­ bility in Y. lipolytica by CRISPRi, fatty acid induction promoter, and flavonoid induction heterozygous promoter, resulting in a flavonoid-producing phenotype that was sustained for up to 320 gen­ erations (Lv et al., 2020). In addition, to maintain the balance of metabolic flux and better coordinate the relationship between cell growth and the high product yield, a quorum-sensing (QS)-based regu­ lation tool for independently regulating multiple metabolic pathways was developed (Dinh and Prather, 2019), combining components of the lux and esa QS systems to simultaneously and dynamically upregulate and downregulate the expression of two sets of genes and successfully address challenges in both the naringenin and salicylic acid synthesis pathways. Overall, mathematical model and dynamic regulation are powerful tools to coordinate the conflict among various metabolic pathways and the conflict between cell growth and production in MCFs.
## 3.4. Selection and collaboration of host cells
Selecting an efficient host cell with efficient and specific protein production or metabolic pathways that can synthesize plant flavonoids has proven to be an effective strategy to cope with these challenges (Table 1)(Delmulle et al., 2018; Marsafari et al., 2020). Among them, Y. lipolytica was used as the main host cell to synthesize various natural products, including polyunsaturated fatty acids, flavonoids, and isoprene, because of its vigorous cell growth and high acetyl-CoA and malonyl-CoA flux. Moreover, as an oil-producing yeast, it can synthesize a large number of liposomes and have rich membrane structures and a lipophilic environment, providing an ideal environment for the regio­ selectivity and stereoselectivity of many plant-derived cytochrome P450 enzymes (Muhammad et al., 2020). Taking advantage of the powerful characteristics of Y. lipolytica, liquiritigenin was synthesized at a final titer of $62.4~\mathrm{mg/L}$ (Akram et al., 2021). In addition, S. cerevisiae is generally recognized as safe, has a clear genetic background, and is widely used in the production of plant flavonoids. For example, based on the co-expression of multiple hydroxylases in S. cerevisiae, $246.4~\mathrm{mg/L}$ dihydromyricetin with a total flavonoid of $528.9\mathrm{mg/L}$ was obtained in a $^{5\mathrm{~L~}}$ bioreactor (Li et al., 2020) and the de novo synthesis of baicalein, baicalin, norwogonin, wogonin, isowogonin and moslosooflavone in S. cerevisiae was realized by establishing the production platform of chrysin (Liu et al., 2020). Similarly, many other excellent hosts have been selected to synthesize plant flavonoids (Table 1). Meanwhile, collaboration between many strains (modular co-culture engineering) has been used to reduce the metabolic burden of cells and the conflict between cell growth and production, and cope with the challenges of the unbalanced metabolic flux among various pathways. Modular co-culture engineering is a method that allows multiple species of microbes to grow together to facilitate a divide-and-conquer approach for chemical biosynthesis and to monitor and control the population dynamics of each strain module, increasing the metabolic flux toward the synthesis
## Efficient metabolic pathway
of the target products (Fig. 6)(Jones and Wang, 2018; Wang et al., 2020a). It has been widely used in the MCFs of different organisms (Table 3). For example, a co-culture system of $E$ . coli was constructed by segmenting the long metabolic pathway in different strains and opti­ mizing each strain module, reducing the metabolic burden of host cells and ensuring the balance of metabolic flux (Wang et al., 2020a). Using a co-culture system of E. coli, bisdemethoxycurcumin (Fang et al., 2018), anthocyanins (Jones et al., 2017), pyranoanthocyanins (Akdemir et al., 2019b), and sakuranetin (Wang et al., 2020b) were synthesized and a screening method based on high-throughput flow cytometry was developed for the identification of naringenin-overproducing E. coli strains (Xiu et al., 2017). In addition, a S. cerevisiae co-culture platform (Du et al., 2020)suitable for the production of various flavonoids was designed to reduce the metabolic burden of host cells (Jones et al., 2016b)and promote the production of other plant flavonoids with more complex structures. Therefore, the selection and collaboration of host cells are important for improving the catalytic activity of key enzymes, reducing metabolic burden to balance metabolic flux, and coordinating the conflict between cell growth and production.
## 4. Artificial intelligence creates opportunities for solving these challenges
With the emergence of big data, an increasing number of computer tools are being developed to cope with various challenges in MCFs. AlphaFold2, de novo protein design, biocatalytic retrosynthesis, artificial intelligence, and intelligent stress resistance have set off a wave of research on biological big data, providing more opportunities for the high efficient production of plant flavonoids in MCFs.
## 4.1. Structure prediction and de novo protein design
Precise protein structure (Callaway, 2020) is the foundation of pro­ tein engineering, and currently, AlphaFold2 (Tunyasuvunakool et al., 2021) is the hottest tool for protein structure prediction. The DeepMind team’s AlphaFold2 beat a group of competitors in CASP14 and reached an unprecedented level of accuracy in protein structure prediction. The solution of the 50-year-old problem of protein folding was described as a "revolutionary" breakthrough in structural biology and a milestone in protein research. The breakthrough research of AlphaFold2 would pave the way for protein engineering. Moreover, de novo protein design can completely design new proteins according to the needs of the research. Designing proteins with specific spatial structures and expected func­ tions has become a new key technology in the process of protein function research. In recent years, considerable progress has been made in designing stable proteins based on the principle of protein folding to the lowest free energy state. These efforts were mainly focused on maxi­ mizing the free energy gap between the desired structure and all other structures, producing a variety of unique stable proteins (Huang et al., 2014, 2016). David Baker’s team (Koepnick et al., 2019) optimized the protein design process, enabling even the non-professional researchers to accurately design a new protein structure and making protein design more widely used. Therefore, according to the existing calculation methods and tools, designing a specific functional protein and predicting the crystal structure of the protein will be an important method for improving the catalytic activity and specificity of enzymes and analyzing the catalytic mechanism, providing new opportunities for the high efficient synthesis of plant flavonoids in MCFs.
## 4.2. Biocatalytic retrosynthesis and artificial intelligence
Since the end of the 1960s, the pioneering work of Professor E. J. Corey and his collaborators (Corey et al., 1985) laid the foundation for computational retrosynthetic analysis. By applying the same basic principles, computer algorithms can greatly improve the speed and ef­ ficiency. With the rise of artificial intelligence, new machine learning technologies are redefining the process of retrosynthetic planning, and many retrosynthetic algorithms based on deep learning are emerging. In the framework of Bayesian reasoning and calculation, high-precision forward predictions can be made for the combination of given re­ actants through a training of a deep neural network (Guo et al., 2020). Using the Bayesian conditional probability theorem and inverting the forward model into the reverse model, the synthesis route from specific molecules to commercial compounds can be found (Guo et al., 2020). Through manual mining of more than 250,000 publications, more than 100,000 biochemical reactions, routes, and biosynthesis data links were integrated to develop the RxnFinder (Hu et al., 2011) online tool that met the query of most biochemical reactions and saved the time and energy of manual searching. However, these algorithms rely on the integration and manual mining of the database and require considerable manpower and material resources to be updated. The database is still relatively small, especially for complex natural products, resulting in inaccurate predictions. In recent years, new artificial intelligence (AI) technologies (Lin et al., 2020) have emerged in the fields of chemistry and pharmacy and have been gradually used to predict chemical reac­ tion products and perform retrosynthetic analysis, which is expected to break through the bottleneck of looking for efficient enzymes.
## 4.3. Intelligent stress resistance
The biological fermentation process is a key and difficult point to realize the industrialization of producing plant flavonoids by MCFs. However, high cost and high energy consumption have gradually become the characteristics of the industry owing to the factors of heat production, pH change, enzyme catalysis, and raw material products. So far, the Bayesian modeling approach (Li et al., 2021) and synthetic biology techniques (Jia et al., 2016; Xu et al., 2018, 2020a) have been used to enhance MCFs’ self-defense systems and design and integrate reprogrammed MCFs. For example, a novel approach was developed to incorporate temperature dependence into an enzyme-constrained GEM (ecGEM) with the resulting model termed enzyme and temperature-constrained GEM (etcGEM)(S´anchez et al., 2017). The approach combines the following steps: (i) etcGEM construction, (ii) flux balance analysis (FBA), and (iii) Bayesian statistical learning. Bayesian modeling greatly improved the etcGEM performance by reducing the parameter statistical uncertainties. Squalene epoxidase (ERG1) was predicted to be the most rate-limiting. By replacing this key enzyme with a homologue of a thermotolerant yeast strain, a thermotolerant strain that outperforms the wild type was obtained, demonstrating the key role of sterol metabolism in yeast thermosensitivity (Li et al., 2021). How­ ever, research in this field is still in its initial stage and has not been widely used in all MCFs. The signal transduction modes of multiple stress factors in the fermentation and production of plant flavonoids are not completely clear. This is an important reason for restricting the development of stress signal transduction. Therefore, to understand the metabolic regulation of MCFs, it is important to study the mechanism of stress resistance at the molecular level and to clarify the molecular mechanism of blocking the cascade conduction of MCFs stress. This will improve the resistance of MCFs in multiple pressure environments, greatly improve the production efficiency, reduce fermentation energy consumption and production cost, and realize the efficiency of artificial biological response systems.
## 5. Conclusion
In this paper, two primary challenges are summarized in MCFs: the unbalanced carbon flux among various pathways and the conflict be­ tween growth and production. The main reasons for these two chal­ lenges were analyzed, including lack of enzymes with high activity, high specificity, and efficient metabolic pathways; conflict between the het­ erologous metabolic pathway and the endogenous metabolic network; metabolic burden; overexpression of heterologous or endogenous metabolic pathways and accumulation of heterologous proteins, pathway intermediates, and product; and host with low synthesis abil­ ity. In addition, the strategies developed in recent years to cope with the challenges were summarized in terms of enzymes, pathways, metabolic networks, and host cells, including genome and transcriptome data analysis, protein engineering, promoter engineering, cofactor engi­ neering, coenzyme engineering, metabolic network model, stoichio­ metric model, dynamic regulation, selection of the host cells, and modular co-culture engineering. Finally, the opportunities provided by protein structure prediction, de novo protein design, artificial intelli­ gence (AI), biocatalytic retrosynthesis, and intelligent stress resistance were explored, providing new insights into the high efficient production of plant flavonoids and other natural plant products in MCFs.
